    "result_text": "All 10 tests pass (build succeeds). The change is complete and green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI moved all date-derived values onto a fiscal-year basis (fiscal year starts **1 July**, named by the calendar year it ends in \u2014 e.g. `2025-07-01`\u2026`2026-06-30` \u2192 FY2026).\n\n**New shared helper** \u2014 `util/FiscalYear.java`\n- `of(LocalDate)` \u2192 the fiscal-year number; `label(LocalDate)` \u2192 the `FY<YY>` string. Pure date arithmetic, matching the existing `BusinessDay` helper style. This is the single place the \"starts 1 July\" rule lives.\n\n**`fiscalYear` field** (the new spec output)\n- `OwnerMapper.fiscalYear(owner)` returns `FY<YY>` from the (already business-day-adjusted) registration date, wired via a `@Mapping` expression like the other derived fields.\n- Added the `fiscalYear` schema to `openapi.yml` so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it.\n\n**Changed behaviour, as required:**\n- `MembershipNumber` \u2014 the `-M<YY>` year segment now uses `FiscalYear.of(...)` instead of the raw calendar year.\n- `Tenure` \u2014 replaced `days(...)` (365-day count to `now`) with `fiscalYears(...)`, counting elapsed fiscal-year boundaries; a newly-registered owner is still `0`.\n- `MembershipPoints` \u2014 the tenure bonus now triggers on `Tenure.fiscalYears(owner) >= 1` (constant renamed `TENURE_DAYS` \u2192 `TENURE_FISCAL_YEARS`) instead of the 365-day window.\n\nEach unit kept its single responsibility (fiscal-year math in `FiscalYear`, owner-null handling in the callers), and docs/OpenAPI descriptions were updated to match. `./mvnw test`: **10 run, 0 failures**, including the new `coreReturnsFiscalYear` acceptance test.\n\nNote: the acceptance test only asserts the `FY\\d{2}` format, so I chose the standard Australian convention (fiscal year named by its ending calendar year). If your convention names it by the starting year instead, that's a one-line change in `FiscalYear.of`.",
